<aside><h1>Web Based Parking</h1><p>Sign-in begins at S-1. Google OAuth returns through a server-side callback; after verification, employees use the employee shell and Parking Administrators use the administrator shell. Employees with a dedicated space manage it in S-2 and S-3; eligible employees claim in S-4; employees without a registered plate must add one in S-5 before parking operations. Administrators manage profiles in S-6 and S-7, monitor weekly utilization in S-8, review entrance attempts in S-9, and configure parking and roles in S-10. Every screen is the same responsive web app at phone, tablet, and desktop widths; narrow layouts use touch-sized controls and never a native-app frame.

DESIGN.md / shared design direction: redraw every page in one consistent light interface, with a shared header per user type, one type scale, one restrained accent selected by the design system, compact tables, hairline dividers, clear status labels, and orderly HR-style people lists. Use developer-tool density for logs and reporting: plain bars, compact filters, sortable tables, and one headline metric with context above data. Use real employee and vehicle photographs where requested below. Constrain content to a centered max-width; use CSS Grid rather than percentage math; keep every item in its own non-overlapping zone. NEVER DO: emojis; Inter; generic serif fonts such as Times New Roman, Georgia, or Garamond; pure black; neon or outer-glow shadows; oversaturated accents; excessive gradient text; custom cursors; overlapping or absolutely stacked content; three equal-column cards; generic names; fake round-number metrics; AI clichés such as “Elevate,” “Seamless,” “Unleash,” or “Next-Gen”; filler scroll text or bouncing chevrons; broken Unsplash links; centered high-variance heroes. NEVER use a marketing hero, a row of three equal cards, icon-in-rounded-square feature cards, logo strips, generic KPI cards with sparklines, bento tiles, glass surfaces, or repeated uniform section templates. Replace them with a left-aligned page heading, one contextual headline measure, dense lists or tables, real product/vehicle crops where relevant, and varied but orderly section rhythm. Do not add pricing, testimonials, a marketing footer, version chips, system labels, or fields/devices absent from the requirements and sample data. The same user type sees the same menu labels and order on every page in its shell.</p><h3>Screens</h3><ul><li><a href="#S-1">S-1 <code>/sign-in</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-2">S-2 <code>/employee/my-space</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-3">S-3 <code>/employee/release-dates</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-4">S-4 <code>/employee/free-spaces</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-5">S-5 <code>/employee/my-vehicles</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-6">S-6 <code>/admin/employees</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-7">S-7 <code>/admin/employees/:employeeId</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-8">S-8 <code>/admin/utilization</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-9">S-9 <code>/admin/entry-checks</code></a></li><li><a href="#S-10">S-10 <code>/admin/settings</code></a></li></ul><h3>No screen</h3><ul><li><b>REQ-1</b> When a person submits a Google OAuth callback, the Folio3 Parking web app shall create an authenticated session only after validating the Google-issued ID token signature, issuer, and audience, verifying OAuth state, and confirming that the account belongs to an active Folio3 employee. <i>(no screen: OAuth token signature, issuer, audience, state validation and authenticated-session creation happen in the server-side callback; S-1 is the user-facing sign-in and failure surface, not a separate callback page.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-5</b> Where deployment bootstrap configuration is used, the Folio3 Parking web app shall keep initial administrator setup incomplete until configuration supplies a designated active Folio3 account, then grant that account the initial Parking Administrator role, and allow Parking Administrators to configure the daily release time, per-floor space counts, work calendar, named report recipients, and administrator and Security Guard role assignments. <i>(no screen: The initial bootstrap designation is supplied by deployment configuration, not a user-facing page. S-10 shows its completed status and provides the requested ongoing administrator settings and role assignments.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-14</b> When a released parking allocation becomes claimable, the Folio3 Parking web app shall send an email notification using a fixed template to eligible employees without dedicated parking who have a registered vehicle. <i>(no screen: The fixed-template notification is an outbound email sent when an allocation becomes claimable; it has no page of its own.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-15</b> If a permanently stickered vehicle arrives, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall leave its existing sticker process unaffected and preserve any temporary claim already made for that date, including when configured capacity is exceeded. <i>(no screen: Preserving the separate permanent-sticker process and existing temporary claims during capacity overage is an integration/business rule, not a user-facing page.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-16</b> When the Folio3 LPR integration submits an entrance-verification request, the Folio3 Parking web app shall authenticate the request as originating from trusted Folio3 LPR equipment, require a unique authenticated request ID or nonce and reject any previously accepted ID or nonce, then verify a detected car&#39;s readable plate against that date&#39;s temporary parking authorizations and activate the green indicator only for a valid authorization. <i>(no screen: Trusted-equipment authentication, unique request ID or nonce enforcement, replay rejection, plate authorization, and the hardware indicator command are server-side LPR integration behavior. S-9 displays the resulting attempt records.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-17</b> If any entrance verification fails, then the Folio3 Parking web app shall issue an explicit command to set the green indicator inactive and deny entry through the temporary-parking process. <i>(no screen: The explicit command to set the green indicator inactive and deny temporary-process entry is issued by the LPR integration on verification failure; S-9 displays those outcomes.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-19</b> The Folio3 Parking web app shall record daily utilization events using registered-vehicle entry records, counting an allocation as used when any vehicle registered to its employee is detected that day. <i>(no screen: Daily utilization-event creation from registered-vehicle entry records is automatic backend processing; S-8 presents the resulting records and report.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-21</b> Where a Parking Administrator has configured a named report-recipient list, the Folio3 Parking web app shall send weekly employee-identifying report emails only to configured recipients who have the Parking Administrator role, omitting all other listed addresses from delivery. <i>(no screen: Filtering the configured recipient list to Parking Administrators and sending employee-identifying weekly report emails is outbound email delivery, not a separate screen; S-10 manages the named list.)</i></li><li><b>REQ-23</b> The Folio3 Parking web app shall retain parking-release audit history, LPR verification attempts, and utilization events according to Folio3&#39;s existing records-retention policy. <i>(no screen: Retention follows Folio3&#39;s existing records-retention policy automatically and is not configured on a new page; S-8 and S-9 identify that policy for the records shown.)</i></li></ul></aside>
